    slug: "re-baselining-ad-measurement-2026",
    title: "Your Conversions Did Not Drop, Your Measurement Changed: How to Re-Baseline Reporting",
    excerpt:
      "Attribution definitions shifted on both major platforms. If your numbers moved without a campaign change, here is how to tell a measurement artefact from a real performance problem.",
    category: "Tracking & Analytics",
    readTime: "9 min read",
    date: "September 2026",
    gradient: "from-violet-500 to-purple-500",
    content: [
      "A client called me convinced their account had collapsed. Conversions were down sharply month on month, nothing in the campaigns had changed, and they were ready to pause everything.",
      "Revenue in their accounting software was flat. The campaigns were fine. The definition of a conversion had changed underneath them.",
      "## The Problem: A Moving Yardstick",
      "Measuring performance requires the measuring instrument to stay still. When a platform changes how it counts, every comparison spanning that change becomes invalid, and dashboards do not warn you.",
      "The most consequential recent change is Meta's, announced 3 March 2026. Two things moved at once: click-through now counts only link clicks, with other interaction types shifted into a one-day engaged-view column, and the video engagement threshold that qualifies as an interaction dropped from ten seconds to five.",
      "The first change reduces reported click-through conversions. The second increases reported engaged-view conversions. The net effect varies by account, but almost every account's numbers moved without any change in real performance.",
      "> If your reported conversions changed and your bank deposits did not, you are looking at a measurement artefact, not a business problem. Investigate the dashboard before you touch the campaigns.",
      "## Understanding Why This Keeps Happening",
      "Attribution is not a fact. It is a modelled opinion about which touchpoint deserves credit, and the model is revised regularly.",
      "Three forces drive the churn. Privacy restrictions keep removing the signals attribution was built on. Platforms keep moving toward modelled conversions that estimate rather than observe. And every platform has a commercial incentive to count generously within its own walls.",
      "None of this makes platform reporting useless. It makes it a relative instrument rather than an absolute one, and it means any trend line spanning a definitional change needs a marker on it.",
      `<svg viewBox="0 0 760 210" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Reported conversions appear to fall at a definition change while actual revenue stays flat"><rect width="760" height="210" fill="none"/><line x1="60" y1="170" x2="720" y2="170" stroke="#475569" stroke-width="1.5"/><line x1="60" y1="25" x2="60" y2="170" stroke="#475569" stroke-width="1.5"/><line x1="390" y1="25" x2="390" y2="170" stroke="#a855f7" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 4"/><text x="390" y="18" fill="#c4b5fd" font-size="12" font-family="sans-serif" text-anchor="middle">Definition changed</text><polyline points="80,80 150,76 220,82 290,78 360,80 390,80 420,124 490,120 560,126 630,122 700,124" fill="none" stroke="#ef4444" stroke-width="2.5"/><polyline points="80,110 150,108 220,112 290,107 360,110 390,109 420,110 490,108 560,111 630,107 700,109" fill="none" stroke="#22c55e" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-dasharray="5 3"/><text x="190" y="66" fill="#fca5a5" font-size="12" font-family="sans-serif">Platform-reported conversions</text><text x="150" y="134" fill="#86efac" font-size="12" font-family="sans-serif">Actual revenue (flat)</text><text x="560" y="100" fill="#fca5a5" font-size="12" font-family="sans-serif">Looks like a 35% drop</text></svg>`,
      "## The Approach: Build a Reference That Does Not Move",
      "The defence is simple in principle and rarely implemented: keep one measurement source the platforms do not control.",
      "Your commerce platform or accounting software is that source. Revenue recorded there does not change because an ad platform revised its attribution model. Compare total revenue against total spend, and you have a trend line immune to definitional drift.",
      "Alongside it, keep an annotation log. Every time a platform announces a measurement change, write the date and what changed. When someone asks why conversions moved in March, the answer takes thirty seconds instead of a week.",
      "For lead generation, close the loop with offline conversion imports. Sending qualified and closed-won status back to the platform means you are optimising toward revenue rather than form fills, and gives you a quality measure that survives attribution changes entirely.",
      "## Distinguishing an Artefact From a Real Problem",
      "Four checks separate the two, in order of speed:",
      "1. Did total revenue in your own records move? If not, the campaigns are fine.",
      "2. Did the change coincide with an announced platform update? Check your annotation log.",
      "3. Did impressions, clicks and click-through rate move, or only conversions? Upstream metrics unchanged with downstream metrics falling points at measurement, not delivery.",
      "4. Did every campaign move together by a similar proportion? Real performance problems are usually concentrated. Measurement changes hit everything at once.",
      "## The Solution: Reporting That Survives the Next Change",
      "1. Make revenue from your own records the headline figure in every report.",
      "2. Keep a dated annotation log of platform measurement changes and mark them on your charts.",
      "3. Fix your attribution window and never change it mid-analysis.",
      "4. Report a consistent set of upstream metrics, impressions, clicks and click-through rate, so you can separate delivery problems from counting problems.",
      "5. Implement offline conversion imports for lead generation so quality, not volume, drives optimisation.",
      "6. Before reacting to any sharp change, run the four checks above. Most sharp changes are not what they appear to be.",
      "> The discipline is not distrusting platform data. It is knowing which number to check first when two sources disagree, and it should always be the one that comes from your own books.",
      "## Frequently Asked Questions",
      "?? Why did my Meta conversions drop suddenly with no campaign changes? | Most likely a measurement change rather than a performance change. From the announcement dated 3 March 2026, click-through counts only link clicks, with other interactions moved to a one-day engaged-view column, and the video engagement threshold fell from ten seconds to five.",
      "?? How do I tell a tracking problem from a performance problem? | Check whether revenue in your own records moved. Then check whether impressions, clicks and click-through rate moved, or only conversions. Upstream metrics holding steady while conversions fall points to measurement rather than delivery.",
      "?? Should I change my attribution window? | Not during an analysis. Pick a window appropriate to your purchase cycle and keep it fixed. Changing it mid-analysis makes before-and-after comparison meaningless, because you are asking two different questions.",
      "?? What are offline conversion imports and why do they matter? | They send outcomes from your CRM, such as qualified or closed-won status, back to the ad platform. This lets optimisation target leads that become customers rather than leads that merely fill in a form, and the quality signal is unaffected by attribution changes.",
      "?? What is the most reliable number in paid advertising? | Total revenue from your own commerce or accounting system, divided by total advertising spend. Both figures come from your records, so no platform revision can inflate or deflate the result.",
    ],
